1
00:01:21,949 --> 00:01:23,110
Okay, let's go.

2
00:01:23,283 --> 00:01:25,445
Get two, judge, get two.

3
00:01:25,619 --> 00:01:27,747
Come on, judge!

4
00:01:28,288 --> 00:01:29,586
All right!

5
00:01:29,756 --> 00:01:32,021
Good arms. Good
arms. Good throw.

6
00:01:32,192 --> 00:01:33,660
All right, that's
the way to shoot!

7
00:01:33,827 --> 00:01:35,796
Nice going, judge.

8
00:01:41,134 --> 00:01:43,035
You okay, Ben?

9
00:01:44,638 --> 00:01:47,233
You said you'd give
me a shot at third base.

10
00:01:47,808 --> 00:01:50,368
Well, yeah, but that was before
Judge Edwards said he'd play.

11
00:01:50,544 --> 00:01:52,604
He's played semi-pro, Ben.

12
00:01:52,779 --> 00:01:55,044
You promised
you'd give me a shot.

13
00:01:56,683 --> 00:01:59,812
Judge, trade places with
Ben for a minute. Okay?

14
00:01:59,987 --> 00:02:02,513
- Okay?
- Thanks.

15
00:02:07,861 --> 00:02:10,092
Come on, Bob, put
a little pepper on it.

16
00:02:10,364 --> 00:02:12,196
Go, Ben. Whoo!

17
00:02:12,766 --> 00:02:16,066
- Get one, Ben.
- Come on, Ben.

18
00:02:16,236 --> 00:02:19,570
- That's okay, Ben!
- Come on, Ben. Stay low.

19
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:22,232
- Keep your eye on the ball. Okay?
- The sun was in my eyes.

20
00:02:22,409 --> 00:02:24,071
Give me another one, Bob.

21
00:02:28,281 --> 00:02:29,408
Ugh.

22
00:02:31,418 --> 00:02:33,649
It was his ball.

23
00:02:45,499 --> 00:02:47,365
Nick, get off that bike now!

24
00:02:52,606 --> 00:02:54,973
Don't make me hurt you.

25
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,076
- Book him, Jim.
- Nice going, Bob. Let's go!

26
00:03:04,584 --> 00:03:06,553
- Nice wheels, Bob.
- Yeah.

27
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:07,744
You all right?

28
00:03:07,921 --> 00:03:11,323
Boy, I haven't moved that fast
since that buffet couple of weeks ago.

29
00:03:11,491 --> 00:03:15,223
- You sure you're all right?
- Oh, yeah, I'm okay. I'm all right.

30
00:03:15,395 --> 00:03:17,075
Second time I've
booked that kid this year.

31
00:03:17,230 --> 00:03:19,392
Think he'd find a
game he'd be better at.

32
00:03:19,566 --> 00:03:23,128
Hey, you mark my words, he'll
be back on the street tomorrow.

33
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:40,987
- You sure you're all right, Bob?
- Oh, it's just a cut, Ben. I'm fine.

34
00:03:41,154 --> 00:03:44,454
Listen, I need to talk to you
about this third base position.

35
00:03:44,624 --> 00:03:47,253
Me too. You're right.

36
00:03:47,427 --> 00:03:50,488
If Wayne Edwards played
semi-pro, he should play third base.

37
00:03:50,664 --> 00:03:54,328
- I'm glad you feel that way, Ben.
- I'll play right field.

38
00:03:54,501 --> 00:03:57,164
You know, a right fielder
needs a really good arm.

39
00:03:57,337 --> 00:03:59,533
- I've got a good arm.
- No, you don't, Ben.

40
00:03:59,706 --> 00:04:01,106
You throw like a girl.

41
00:04:06,747 --> 00:04:08,579
But you've got the best
mind on the team, Ben,

42
00:04:08,749 --> 00:04:10,615
and you'd make a
great third base coach.

43
00:04:14,488 --> 00:04:16,855
I'm sorry. I didn't mean
to hurt your feelings.

44
00:04:18,024 --> 00:04:21,722
You didn't hurt my
feelings. It's just a game.

45
00:04:23,330 --> 00:04:26,198
Well, back to business.

46
00:04:26,366 --> 00:04:28,631
- No, see, that's what I mean.
- What?

47
00:04:28,802 --> 00:04:31,237
Back to business. You've
got the best mind on the team.

48
00:04:32,339 --> 00:04:35,706
Well... You know Malcolm Engle?

49
00:04:36,143 --> 00:04:39,079
I've heard of him. He's the
millionaire philanthropist?

50
00:04:40,113 --> 00:04:43,914
I guess he wants me to
do a little legal work for him.

51
00:04:44,985 --> 00:04:47,454
Hope he doesn't find
out I throw like a girl.

52
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:08,666
Do you like DiCenzo,
Mr. Matlock?

53
00:05:11,244 --> 00:05:13,770
You have expensive tastes.

54
00:05:13,947 --> 00:05:17,440
Well, Mr. Engle, I'm
glad to finally meet you.

55
00:05:17,617 --> 00:05:19,347
I wish we could've met earlier.

56
00:05:19,519 --> 00:05:23,923
Well, the press calls me reclusive,
but I think of myself as private.

57
00:05:24,090 --> 00:05:27,686
Oh, yeah. I was just reading
in the paper this morning

58
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:30,023
about that $2 million endowment

59
00:05:30,197 --> 00:05:33,167
you gave the pediatric
hospital. That's a lot of money.

60
00:05:33,333 --> 00:05:36,462
Well, it's easy to give to
others when you have money.

61
00:05:36,636 --> 00:05:37,934
Well, yeah, but...

62
00:05:38,104 --> 00:05:40,972
Your favorite charity is
the Legal Defense Fund.

63
00:05:41,141 --> 00:05:43,110
Am I right?

64
00:05:43,276 --> 00:05:45,268
How in the world
did you know that?

65
00:05:45,445 --> 00:05:51,009
I've taken the liberty of writing a check
for $25,000 to that fund in your name.

66
00:05:52,819 --> 00:05:54,811
Get out of town.

67
00:05:55,856 --> 00:05:58,052
I hope I can do
something in return.

68
00:05:58,225 --> 00:06:02,629
Well, for starters, you can agree to be
my guest here for dinner this evening.

69
00:06:03,496 --> 00:06:05,362
Well...

70
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:07,752
I've had my chef
prepare your favorite dish,

71
00:06:07,901 --> 00:06:11,963
which is venison stew,
two cloves of garlic.

72
00:06:12,138 --> 00:06:15,404
Vidalia onions. I
hope you'll stay.

73
00:06:15,876 --> 00:06:18,004
I love Vidalia onions.

74
00:06:19,246 --> 00:06:20,908
Oh...

75
00:06:22,048 --> 00:06:26,281
I could eat this stew every
night and die a happy man.

76
00:06:26,453 --> 00:06:28,945
I'd be as big as a
house, but I'd die happy.

77
00:06:30,390 --> 00:06:34,327
It's a pleasure to see a man
passionate about something.

78
00:06:36,596 --> 00:06:38,428
Well...

79
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:44,831
Well, I don't want you to
do any legal work for me.

80
00:06:46,873 --> 00:06:48,273
Say again?

81
00:06:48,441 --> 00:06:51,275
I was going to, but then
my assistant showed me

82
00:06:51,444 --> 00:06:54,175
the transcript from
the Galloway case.

83
00:06:54,948 --> 00:06:57,315
- Fletcher Galloway?
- Yeah.

84
00:06:57,484 --> 00:07:01,114
Boy, did you ever stink
up that case. Ha, ha, ha.

85
00:07:02,856 --> 00:07:03,915
I don't get it.

86
00:07:04,958 --> 00:07:07,587
You can't exactly blame
that one on age, can you?

87
00:07:07,761 --> 00:07:09,389
I mean, that happened
what, 20 years ago?

88
00:07:10,130 --> 00:07:11,154
What went wrong anyway?

89
00:07:11,331 --> 00:07:13,300
You just didn't do your
homework or something?

90
00:07:14,701 --> 00:07:18,103
Mr. Engle, I don't
find this very funny.

91
00:07:19,739 --> 00:07:23,232
I was wondering how you actually
sleep at night knowing that your client,

92
00:07:23,410 --> 00:07:29,247
James Johnson, is sitting on
death row because of your ineptness.

93
00:07:29,649 --> 00:07:32,380
James Johnson
committed a murder.

94
00:07:32,552 --> 00:07:34,544
He lied to me and
he lied to the jury.

95
00:07:34,721 --> 00:07:38,817
Oh, well, I was wondering how
you rationalized bailing out on him.

96
00:07:41,594 --> 00:07:46,658
I don't know what's going
on here or what this is about.

97
00:07:53,807 --> 00:07:55,105
Thanks for dinner.

98
00:08:08,822 --> 00:08:10,188
Open the door.

99
00:08:11,524 --> 00:08:13,789
I haven't excused you yet.

100
00:08:17,163 --> 00:08:23,103
You know, Ben, there are a lot of
people out there like James Johnson

101
00:08:23,269 --> 00:08:25,500
who think that yours
is a great criminal mind.

102
00:08:25,672 --> 00:08:30,133
But do you know what I
think? I think you're a fraud.

103
00:08:30,310 --> 00:08:34,111
I think you couldn't get a guy off
even if the real killer described to you,

104
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:37,307
in advance, how
he planned to do it.

105
00:08:38,818 --> 00:08:43,847
I've listened to all of this I can
handle. Now unlock that door.

106
00:08:44,324 --> 00:08:48,193
A murder is gonna take place,
Ben. And I'm gonna give it all to you.

107
00:08:48,361 --> 00:08:50,626
I'm gonna tell you where.
I'm gonna tell you when.

108
00:08:50,797 --> 00:08:53,631
I'm even gonna tell
you who's gonna do it.

109
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:55,893
Me.

110
00:08:59,606 --> 00:09:01,973
You're telling me you're
gonna kill somebody?

111
00:09:02,409 --> 00:09:04,105
That's right.

112
00:09:05,211 --> 00:09:07,180
You can go now.

113
00:09:17,057 --> 00:09:20,687
Three-fifteen tomorrow.
Willow Springs Park.

114
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:23,489
Someone very close to you, Ben.

115
00:09:26,366 --> 00:09:28,961
Catch me if you can.

116
00:09:48,455 --> 00:09:51,289
Malcolm Engle is gonna
kill someone today?

117
00:09:51,458 --> 00:09:54,485
- In six hours.
- Malcolm Engle?

118
00:09:54,661 --> 00:09:57,688
A man who gave $20
million to charity last year,

119
00:09:57,864 --> 00:10:00,527
was voted Humanitarian of
the Year for the state of Georgia

120
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:06,162
for the third consecutive year,
created the Be a Brother Foundation,

121
00:10:06,339 --> 00:10:10,640
invited you to his house to tell you
that he'd decided to kill somebody?

122
00:10:10,810 --> 00:10:12,403
- That's right.
- Why?

123
00:10:12,579 --> 00:10:13,706
I don't know.

124
00:10:14,180 --> 00:10:15,739
You must have misinterpreted.

125
00:10:15,915 --> 00:10:19,477
All I know is he said
somebody's gonna die today.

126
00:10:20,653 --> 00:10:23,555
Well, even if I believed
Malcolm Engle was some lunatic

127
00:10:23,723 --> 00:10:26,318
who's gonna do what you
said, my hands are tied.

128
00:10:26,493 --> 00:10:28,052
He hasn't committed a crime.

129
00:10:52,685 --> 00:10:55,280
- Elizabeth?
- Yes, Mr. Engle.

130
00:10:55,455 --> 00:10:57,055
I'm gonna call Mitchell
Goetz in New York

131
00:10:57,223 --> 00:10:59,590
and I do not wanna be
disturbed, so hold all my calls.

132
00:10:59,759 --> 00:11:01,193
Will do.

133
00:11:30,723 --> 00:11:33,022
<i>This is Mitchell Goetz. I'm
not in the office right now,</i>

134
00:11:33,193 --> 00:11:35,992
<i>but you can leave your
message at the tone.</i>

135
00:11:38,698 --> 00:11:40,360
<i>Mitchell, it's Malcolm.</i>

136
00:11:40,533 --> 00:11:44,436
<i>It's a little before 3 here in
Georgia. I'm sorry I missed you.</i>

137
00:11:44,604 --> 00:11:48,632
<i>I spoke with our friend in Chicago
about the mechanics of this deal.</i>

138
00:11:48,808 --> 00:11:51,368
<i>I wanted you to hear the details
before you talk to him tomorrow.</i>

139
00:11:51,511 --> 00:11:53,673
<i>It's a bit complicated.</i>

140
00:11:55,014 --> 00:11:57,483
<i>I hope you have a lot of tape
on this answering machine.</i>

141
00:11:57,650 --> 00:11:59,744
<i>Well, anyway, here goes.</i>

142
00:11:59,919 --> 00:12:04,380
<i>Ziegler Industries had their initial
public offering two years ago in June.</i>

143
00:12:04,557 --> 00:12:07,550
<i>The stock came on at
the market at $22 a share.</i>

144
00:12:07,727 --> 00:12:09,525
<i>Over the course
of the first year</i>

145
00:12:09,696 --> 00:12:11,665
<i>the stock's fluctuation
was negligible</i>

146
00:12:11,831 --> 00:12:13,493
<i>in that the products
they were developing</i>

147
00:12:13,666 --> 00:12:15,498
<i>were in stage one of testing...</i>

148
00:12:19,038 --> 00:12:21,872
Hey, think we should
start batting practice now?

149
00:12:22,041 --> 00:12:24,340
- What?
- Well, Jim McCoy's gotta leave early.

150
00:12:24,510 --> 00:12:26,638
And I don't know, should
we start batting practice?

151
00:12:26,813 --> 00:12:28,714
Yeah, okay. Jerri,
you wanna hit?

152
00:12:28,881 --> 00:12:30,713
Sure.

153
00:12:35,088 --> 00:12:37,751
Hey, thanks a lot, Greg.
I appreciate it, buddy.

154
00:12:54,807 --> 00:12:55,934
<i>Testing.</i>

155
00:12:56,109 --> 00:12:59,876
<i>His guess is that in the short
term, there is a strong likelihood</i>

156
00:13:00,046 --> 00:13:03,141
<i>that we wouldn't see a
return on our investment.</i>

157
00:13:03,316 --> 00:13:05,649
<i>Hey, wait, hang on a second.
I just remembered something.</i>

158
00:13:06,853 --> 00:13:09,413
<i>- Elizabeth.</i>
- Yes, sir.

159
00:13:09,589 --> 00:13:11,820
<i>I'm slipping the Frazier
deal under the door.</i>

160
00:13:11,991 --> 00:13:15,655
<i>Could you execute my
revisions before you go home?</i>

161
00:13:17,263 --> 00:13:18,788
Un-huh.

162
00:13:35,381 --> 00:13:37,282
Hey!

163
00:13:38,551 --> 00:13:42,613
Detective, what brings you
to this neck of the woods?

164
00:13:42,789 --> 00:13:45,190
- You're...
- I'm Malcolm Engle.

165
00:13:46,426 --> 00:13:48,759
Why are you looking
at me like that?

166
00:13:49,362 --> 00:13:52,161
- Well, what are you doing here?
- Oh.

167
00:13:53,032 --> 00:13:56,332
Ha, ha, ha. It's
Matlock, isn't it?

168
00:13:56,502 --> 00:13:59,939
I'll bet he's been telling you some
strange things about me, hasn't he?

169
00:14:01,407 --> 00:14:05,742
- I said what are you doing here?
- Wait, wait, wait, let me guess.

170
00:14:05,912 --> 00:14:10,646
Did he tell you that I was
gonna kill somebody today?

171
00:14:11,951 --> 00:14:15,353
Yeah, as a matter of
fact, he said it'd be about...

172
00:14:18,057 --> 00:14:19,057
Unh!

173
00:14:33,873 --> 00:14:36,570
Come on, Jerri.
Come on, hit it. Whoa!

174
00:14:36,743 --> 00:14:37,767
- Good hit!
- Where's Bob?

175
00:14:37,944 --> 00:14:40,436
- Where is Bob?
- I don't know.

176
00:14:40,613 --> 00:14:43,048
It's not like him to be late.

177
00:14:49,021 --> 00:14:50,781
<i>If you've got any
questions about any of this</i>

178
00:14:50,923 --> 00:14:52,763
<i>and you wanna reach me
before you call Chicago,</i>

179
00:14:52,925 --> 00:14:54,450
<i>I should be home
most of tonight.</i>

180
00:14:54,627 --> 00:14:57,358
<i>Elizabeth knows my schedule
better than I do, so give her a buzz,</i>

181
00:14:57,530 --> 00:14:59,158
<i>and she'll track me down.</i>

182
00:14:59,332 --> 00:15:02,393
<i>I'll talk to you
soon, Larry. Bye.</i>

183
00:15:25,291 --> 00:15:27,226
Any messages while
I was on the phone?

184
00:15:27,393 --> 00:15:29,123
Yes, sir.

185
00:15:30,363 --> 00:15:32,195
- Thank you.
- Mm-hm.

186
00:15:58,858 --> 00:16:01,953
- Hello?
<i>- How was your day, Ben?</i>

187
00:16:02,128 --> 00:16:03,408
<i>I hope it's not too late to call</i>

188
00:16:03,563 --> 00:16:06,727
but I heard about your
good friend, Detective Brooks.

189
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:09,669
<i>Must be very painful for you.</i>

190
00:16:09,836 --> 00:16:12,533
- Why did you do it?
<i>- I heard he died at,</i>

191
00:16:12,705 --> 00:16:17,769
what, about 3:15 today
at Willow Springs Park.

192
00:16:18,411 --> 00:16:22,644
<i>- It's just a game!</i>
- No, I don't play games, Ben.

193
00:16:23,516 --> 00:16:26,008
I hear the police
picked up some punk.

194
00:16:26,185 --> 00:16:31,647
Boy, kids. I just don't know
what's happened to them.

195
00:16:31,824 --> 00:16:36,626
- You murdered my friend!
<i>- Ben, you sound so angry.</i>

196
00:16:36,796 --> 00:16:38,822
<i>Just think.</i>

197
00:16:38,998 --> 00:16:42,730
Maybe if you'd have been smarter,
you could have protected him, Ben.

198
00:16:44,303 --> 00:16:46,829
<i>You should have protected him.</i>

199
00:17:14,567 --> 00:17:18,868
Are you all right? I must
have called you six times.

200
00:17:19,038 --> 00:17:21,371
I didn't know it was you.

201
00:17:28,581 --> 00:17:31,915
I don't know of a person in the
world that I butted heads with

202
00:17:32,084 --> 00:17:36,886
more than Bob Brooks or
anybody I respected more.

203
00:17:46,032 --> 00:17:48,126
I'm so sorry.

204
00:17:55,174 --> 00:17:57,268
Malcolm Engle?

205
00:17:58,444 --> 00:18:00,413
Yeah.

206
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:02,742
Malcolm Engle.

207
00:18:15,394 --> 00:18:16,623
Charlie.

208
00:18:19,865 --> 00:18:21,834
Who are you?

209
00:18:22,501 --> 00:18:23,992
Your lawyer.

210
00:18:24,170 --> 00:18:27,368
I don't want some stupid
public defender in a cheap suit.

211
00:18:28,441 --> 00:18:29,807
Put that cigarette out.

212
00:18:31,110 --> 00:18:33,443
You ever even tried
a murder case, huh?

213
00:18:33,613 --> 00:18:35,104
Put the cigarette out!

214
00:18:35,715 --> 00:18:39,117
Well, what are you gonna
do? You gonna throw me in jail?

215
00:18:40,086 --> 00:18:44,114
Now, let me tell you, for a jerk
who'd have to improve his skills

216
00:18:44,290 --> 00:18:45,383
to be called a thief,

217
00:18:45,558 --> 00:18:48,960
you have no idea what kind
of lucky day you're having.

218
00:18:49,128 --> 00:18:52,690
Now shut your mouth
and put that cigarette out.

219
00:19:03,309 --> 00:19:06,040
Mr. Engle was in the
office all day yesterday.

220
00:19:06,212 --> 00:19:08,204
He didn't go for a walk
or go down the hallway

221
00:19:08,381 --> 00:19:10,247
for about half hour
or so around 3:00?

222
00:19:10,650 --> 00:19:13,279
Um, 3:00. Well, it's
easy enough to check.

223
00:19:13,452 --> 00:19:17,014
Mr. Engle has me keep a
log of all his daily activities. Oh.

224
00:19:18,391 --> 00:19:19,882
Here it is.

225
00:19:20,059 --> 00:19:24,827
Nope, he was on the phone to
New York with Mitchell Goetz.

226
00:19:24,997 --> 00:19:26,295
See, right here.

227
00:19:26,465 --> 00:19:30,300
He got on the phone at 2:55
and he stopped talking at 3:27.

228
00:19:30,469 --> 00:19:32,665
- Are you...? May I take this?
- Oh, sure.

229
00:19:32,838 --> 00:19:34,864
Are you sure these
times are right?

230
00:19:35,041 --> 00:19:36,475
Yes.

231
00:19:36,642 --> 00:19:40,579
Well, as a matter of fact, I remember
that during his phone call to Mr. Goetz,

232
00:19:40,746 --> 00:19:44,410
Mr. Engle slid some papers
under the door for me to revise.

233
00:19:44,583 --> 00:19:46,779
So you never saw him while
he on the phone to New York?

234
00:19:46,952 --> 00:19:48,580
Hello.

235
00:19:48,754 --> 00:19:52,418
Mr. Engle, this is Mr. Lewis
from Mr. Matlock's office.

236
00:19:52,591 --> 00:19:54,560
- How do you do?
- Hi.

237
00:19:54,727 --> 00:19:57,128
I hope that Elizabeth
has been helpful.

238
00:19:57,296 --> 00:19:58,958
Yes, she's been fine.

239
00:19:59,131 --> 00:20:02,158
I got a couple minutes. Do you
wanna come into my office and talk?

240
00:20:02,334 --> 00:20:04,599
- All right.
- Okay.

241
00:20:15,014 --> 00:20:17,074
Can I have Elizabeth get
you some refreshment?

242
00:20:17,249 --> 00:20:20,378
- No.
- Sit down, please.

243
00:20:20,553 --> 00:20:22,146
No thanks, I'm fine.

244
00:20:22,321 --> 00:20:29,057
I was just writing a check to
start a new hospice here in Atlanta.

245
00:20:29,495 --> 00:20:32,465
How could you do it? You
didn't even know Bob Brooks.

246
00:20:32,631 --> 00:20:35,123
There are so many sick people.

247
00:20:35,568 --> 00:20:37,408
You framed some other
guy you didn't even know.

248
00:20:37,570 --> 00:20:39,266
He could end up
getting the electric chair.

249
00:20:39,438 --> 00:20:41,430
Not to mention the homeless.

250
00:20:41,607 --> 00:20:43,803
I wish more people
would care for them.

251
00:20:46,378 --> 00:20:49,075
Your secretary said that you
were in here yesterday on the phone

252
00:20:49,248 --> 00:20:52,912
with Mitchell Goetz
from 2:55 to 3:27.

253
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:59,649
- Hm?
- Mitchell Goetz, 2:55 to 3:27 yesterday.

254
00:20:59,825 --> 00:21:01,623
I want his phone number.

255
00:21:05,131 --> 00:21:08,590
Malcolm Engle never spoke
to Mitchell Goetz that day.

256
00:21:08,768 --> 00:21:11,237
Well, who was he talking to
on the phone for that half hour?

257
00:21:11,403 --> 00:21:14,305
Goetz's telephone
answering machine.

258
00:21:14,473 --> 00:21:16,305
Are you sure?

259
00:21:17,443 --> 00:21:19,844
As it turns out, Mitchell
Goetz was away on business

260
00:21:20,012 --> 00:21:23,141
when Malcolm Engle called and
left a very complicated message.

261
00:21:23,315 --> 00:21:25,580
So, what happened was
Goetz's secretary listened to it

262
00:21:25,751 --> 00:21:27,982
and decided to
jet-air it to Goetz.

263
00:21:28,954 --> 00:21:30,320
Well, how did you get it?

264
00:21:31,791 --> 00:21:35,091
I told Goetz he should jet-air it to me
or I was gonna jet-air him a subpoena.

265
00:21:36,395 --> 00:21:39,763
Now I've talked to cows and
horses that made more sense

266
00:21:39,932 --> 00:21:41,400
than what's on
this tape right here,

267
00:21:41,567 --> 00:21:43,399
but there's something
very interesting on it.

268
00:21:43,569 --> 00:21:45,401
All right now, I've
cued it up to a point

269
00:21:45,571 --> 00:21:47,870
where Engle's on the
phone for about 15 minutes,

270
00:21:48,040 --> 00:21:49,838
and this is where
he supposedly got up

271
00:21:50,009 --> 00:21:52,638
and slid a file under
the door for his secretary.

272
00:21:52,812 --> 00:21:54,678
Just listen.

273
00:21:54,847 --> 00:21:57,180
<i>Hang on a second. I just
remembered something.</i>

274
00:21:57,349 --> 00:22:00,046
<i>Elizabeth? I'm slipping the
Frazier deal under the door.</i>

275
00:22:00,219 --> 00:22:02,745
<i>Could you execute my
revisions before you go home?</i>

276
00:22:02,922 --> 00:22:04,982
And... Now listen.

277
00:22:06,692 --> 00:22:08,160
<i>Sorry, Mitchell. Anyway...</i>

278
00:22:11,897 --> 00:22:14,264
We never heard him get
up and walk over to the door.

279
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:17,626
There was no chair creaking.
There were no footsteps.

280
00:22:17,803 --> 00:22:19,843
It sounded like he was
yelling right into the phone.

281
00:22:19,972 --> 00:22:21,201
Why didn't he move it around?

282
00:22:21,373 --> 00:22:24,366
Do you suppose we
could hear the papers

283
00:22:24,543 --> 00:22:26,205
as he slipped them
under the door?

284
00:22:26,745 --> 00:22:29,510
We didn't. Know what I think?

285
00:22:29,682 --> 00:22:31,742
I think he played a tape
of his voice into the phone

286
00:22:31,917 --> 00:22:35,285
snuck out the back
door, and killed Bob.

287
00:22:51,303 --> 00:22:52,566
Can you see it?

288
00:22:56,342 --> 00:22:58,174
No.

289
00:23:02,848 --> 00:23:05,943
That file could have been
sitting there for 20 minutes.

290
00:23:06,118 --> 00:23:09,816
Unless his secretary got up out
of her desk and walked over here,

291
00:23:09,989 --> 00:23:12,458
there's no way she
would've seen it.

292
00:23:12,625 --> 00:23:16,995
He slid this file under the door
and then slipped out the back.

293
00:23:17,162 --> 00:23:19,825
- That's exactly what he did.
- How did you get in here?

294
00:23:21,467 --> 00:23:25,768
- Well, the door was open and...
- No, it wasn't. I locked it when I left.

295
00:23:25,938 --> 00:23:28,373
Mr. Engle told me why
Mr. Lewis was asking me

296
00:23:28,540 --> 00:23:30,509
all those questions
the other day.

297
00:23:30,676 --> 00:23:32,474
What is wrong with you?

298
00:23:32,645 --> 00:23:35,615
Do you know who this man is
who you're accusing of murder?

299
00:23:36,181 --> 00:23:38,776
- We apologize, apologize...
- Mr. Matlock, do you know

300
00:23:38,951 --> 00:23:40,920
where Mr. Engle was all morning?

301
00:23:41,086 --> 00:23:44,545
This man who you're calling a
killer was feeding terminal patients

302
00:23:44,723 --> 00:23:48,125
at a local hospice
that he funded.

303
00:23:48,294 --> 00:23:50,763
What's going on here?

304
00:23:50,930 --> 00:23:53,798
They broke into the office,
Mr. Engle. I'll call security.

305
00:23:53,966 --> 00:23:57,733
No, that won't be necessary,
Elizabeth. Please leave.

306
00:24:05,044 --> 00:24:06,842
I know how you did it.

307
00:24:07,012 --> 00:24:08,241
You know how I did what?

308
00:24:08,414 --> 00:24:10,246
You never spoke to
Mitchell Goetz directly.

309
00:24:10,416 --> 00:24:12,282
You talked to his
answer machine.

310
00:24:12,451 --> 00:24:14,352
You pre-taped that
call and phoned him

311
00:24:14,520 --> 00:24:16,250
when you knew he'd
be out of his office.

312
00:24:16,422 --> 00:24:20,052
Mr. Matlock, what have I done to you
to warrant this kind of personal attack?

313
00:24:20,626 --> 00:24:24,563
You played that tape over
that phone, walked out that door,

314
00:24:24,730 --> 00:24:28,098
went to the park, killed Bob and
came back here a half hour later.

315
00:24:28,267 --> 00:24:29,565
My God.

316
00:24:29,735 --> 00:24:32,933
I can prove to you right this
second that Mr. Engle was here

317
00:24:33,105 --> 00:24:35,199
when he made that phone call.

318
00:24:35,374 --> 00:24:37,434
We have motion
detectors in this office.

319
00:24:37,609 --> 00:24:38,907
Elizabeth, it's all right.

320
00:24:39,078 --> 00:24:41,445
Look, even if Mr. Engle
had just swiveled in his chair,

321
00:24:41,613 --> 00:24:43,673
well, the red light
would have flashed.

322
00:24:43,849 --> 00:24:45,511
I know this system.

323
00:24:45,684 --> 00:24:48,483
Your security people
will have tapes of that day.

324
00:24:48,654 --> 00:24:50,384
- Give me the tapes.
- Now, that is enough.

325
00:24:50,556 --> 00:24:52,676
- You have tried my patience...
- I can subpoena them.

326
00:24:52,825 --> 00:24:54,623
Give me the tapes!

327
00:24:57,496 --> 00:25:00,330
This panel in our security room
records any movement in the office,

328
00:25:00,499 --> 00:25:02,365
even when the alarm is off.

329
00:25:02,534 --> 00:25:03,866
All right, that's it.

330
00:25:04,036 --> 00:25:07,837
Any time the motion detector
picks up movement in this office,

331
00:25:08,007 --> 00:25:10,567
why, the number one
flashes on the panel

332
00:25:10,743 --> 00:25:13,372
and security knows
someone's here.

333
00:25:13,545 --> 00:25:18,540
- Can you fast forward to about 3:05?
- Oh, sure.

334
00:25:22,388 --> 00:25:23,549
This can't be.

335
00:25:25,057 --> 00:25:26,889
Seen enough?

336
00:25:28,027 --> 00:25:32,624
I don't know how you did
that, but you weren't here.

337
00:25:37,803 --> 00:25:40,830
Could I ask you two ladies
to excuse us for a minute?

338
00:25:50,249 --> 00:25:52,241
You keep trying, Ben.

339
00:25:56,088 --> 00:25:58,853
What is it you old-timers say?

340
00:25:59,024 --> 00:26:03,553
"Even a blind hog finds
an acorn once in a while."

341
00:26:35,260 --> 00:26:37,229
Hello.

342
00:26:38,197 --> 00:26:40,098
Let's call Ben, huh?

343
00:26:40,265 --> 00:26:42,200
Let's call Ben.

344
00:26:49,641 --> 00:26:52,304
Ben? Malcolm Engle.

345
00:26:52,478 --> 00:26:53,639
<i>I thought we should talk.</i>

346
00:26:53,812 --> 00:26:54,836
About what?

347
00:26:55,013 --> 00:26:57,949
<i>I hear you never talk
about your wife. Why is that?</i>

348
00:26:58,550 --> 00:27:00,542
<i>Did you love her a lot?</i>

349
00:27:00,886 --> 00:27:03,481
<i>Is her memory painful to you?</i>

350
00:27:03,655 --> 00:27:05,895
<i>Maybe you think that there's
something you should've said</i>

351
00:27:06,058 --> 00:27:08,425
or done before she died, huh?

352
00:27:08,594 --> 00:27:10,893
How'd you get your
security system to activate

353
00:27:11,063 --> 00:27:13,191
while you were
out murdering Bob?

354
00:27:13,365 --> 00:27:15,960
<i>Oh, Ben, that's so obvious.</i>

355
00:27:16,135 --> 00:27:19,367
<i>Where is that cagey Southern
lawyer who's supposed to trip me up?</i>

356
00:27:19,538 --> 00:27:22,201
Why did you pick Bob, anyway?
You didn't even know him.

357
00:27:22,374 --> 00:27:26,539
Why anyone, Ben? Life isn't
fair. You know that as well as I do.

358
00:27:27,346 --> 00:27:30,043
Seems like life's been
more than fair to you.

359
00:27:30,215 --> 00:27:32,343
Oh, is that so? Heh.

360
00:27:32,518 --> 00:27:34,544
What do you know about me?

361
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:38,179
What do you know about the
price I paid to get where I am, huh?

362
00:27:38,357 --> 00:27:39,985
<i>I know you're evil.</i>

363
00:27:40,159 --> 00:27:42,594
- I know you hate me.
<i>- I'm disappointed.</i>

364
00:27:42,761 --> 00:27:45,026
This isn't as much fun as
I thought it was gonna be.

365
00:27:54,907 --> 00:27:56,876
Do you always eat this much?

366
00:27:57,843 --> 00:27:59,402
Yeah.

367
00:28:03,015 --> 00:28:05,143
Do you ever gain weight?

368
00:28:05,317 --> 00:28:07,309
Why would I do that?

369
00:28:12,658 --> 00:28:17,096
Did you find any connection
between Malcolm Engle and me?

370
00:28:17,262 --> 00:28:20,858
Not in any of your
cases. At least not yet.

371
00:28:25,871 --> 00:28:28,636
It could have been
either one of you.

372
00:28:38,283 --> 00:28:40,047
- Hi.
- Good morning.

373
00:28:40,219 --> 00:28:42,415
Well, I should be
back in about an hour.

374
00:28:42,588 --> 00:28:44,750
- Take your time.
- Okay, I think everything's all set.

375
00:28:44,923 --> 00:28:45,947
- Great.
- Okay.

376
00:28:46,124 --> 00:28:47,888
- I brought a book.
- Oh, good.

377
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:49,790
See you soon.

378
00:29:03,909 --> 00:29:06,936
You can't go in there.
He's in a meeting.

379
00:29:08,614 --> 00:29:11,948
I'm sorry. I'm new. I'm just...
I'm filling in for the regular girl.

380
00:29:15,020 --> 00:29:17,080
Would you look at that.

381
00:29:17,256 --> 00:29:20,283
Oh, that poor little plant
looks like it's going into shock.

382
00:29:20,459 --> 00:29:22,459
- Let me get out of your way.
- Let me take this...

383
00:29:22,594 --> 00:29:24,586
- I can't believe you did that!
- Oh, my goodness!

384
00:29:24,763 --> 00:29:26,443
- Oh, gosh.
- Elizabeth's going to go nuts!

385
00:29:26,598 --> 00:29:28,398
Oh, dear. Well, let me
just take care of this.

386
00:29:39,811 --> 00:29:40,870
How long?

387
00:29:41,046 --> 00:29:43,413
Should be done in
a couple of minutes.

388
00:29:44,483 --> 00:29:46,645
- Ooh. Careful there.
- I'm sorry.

389
00:29:46,818 --> 00:29:48,218
Hey.

390
00:29:50,289 --> 00:29:52,383
- What are you doing?
- Alarm system.

391
00:29:52,557 --> 00:29:54,025
Oh.

392
00:29:54,926 --> 00:29:57,395
- How much is this costing you?
- About 3500.

393
00:29:57,562 --> 00:30:00,191
- You're spending $3500?
- Yeah, what...?

394
00:30:00,365 --> 00:30:02,834
Nothing. It's, you know,
just that usually you're so...

395
00:30:03,001 --> 00:30:06,494
Cheap? This is important.

396
00:30:06,972 --> 00:30:08,167
What'd you find out?

397
00:30:08,340 --> 00:30:11,742
Well, I got a good look at
Mr. Engle's appointment book.

398
00:30:11,910 --> 00:30:13,270
He seems like a
creature of habit.

399
00:30:14,046 --> 00:30:16,515
For the first three months
of the year he played squash

400
00:30:16,682 --> 00:30:19,015
every Saturday at 3:00.

401
00:30:19,184 --> 00:30:21,847
And then he went to Costa
Rica and when he came back,

402
00:30:22,020 --> 00:30:24,080
- he stopped playing squash.
- Huh.

403
00:30:24,256 --> 00:30:28,159
And he stopped getting his hair
cut every other Monday at 11:00.

404
00:30:28,327 --> 00:30:29,989
Maybe he just got a new barber.

405
00:30:30,162 --> 00:30:31,926
But they'd still write
down his appointments.

406
00:30:32,097 --> 00:30:33,690
Oh, right. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

407
00:30:33,865 --> 00:30:37,199
And so far I can't track any of
his movements in Costa Rica.

408
00:30:37,369 --> 00:30:38,860
It's like he was never there.

409
00:30:39,037 --> 00:30:41,563
We're done. Would you
like a little demonstration?

410
00:30:41,740 --> 00:30:43,265
Yeah.

411
00:30:43,442 --> 00:30:45,343
Yeah.

412
00:30:45,510 --> 00:30:49,606
Even though the
system is turned off,

413
00:30:49,781 --> 00:30:52,774
if someone's moving around
in the room, the light still blinks.

414
00:30:52,951 --> 00:30:54,852
Uh-huh. Let me show you.

415
00:30:55,020 --> 00:30:56,386
Jimmy, you stay in there.

416
00:30:57,756 --> 00:31:00,282
Okay. Jimmy, move around.

417
00:31:03,995 --> 00:31:05,861
See? Zone 1.

418
00:31:06,031 --> 00:31:07,124
Don't move.

419
00:31:13,438 --> 00:31:16,636
So somebody's got to be moving
around to make the lights blink.

420
00:31:16,808 --> 00:31:18,401
That's right.

421
00:32:20,071 --> 00:32:21,664
Hello!

422
00:32:21,840 --> 00:32:23,308
What?

423
00:32:23,475 --> 00:32:26,934
What? I can't hear you.

424
00:32:27,579 --> 00:32:30,811
I don't know how to
turn the damn thing off.

425
00:32:31,883 --> 00:32:35,547
What? What do I need a
code for? I'm Ben Matlock.

426
00:32:35,720 --> 00:32:38,656
I live here. I didn't
break in here.

427
00:32:39,925 --> 00:32:44,192
Well, wait a minute. Wait
a minute. Yeah, "hot dog."

428
00:32:44,362 --> 00:32:46,558
That's right, "hot dog!"

429
00:32:46,731 --> 00:32:48,611
The sensors are fine.
I'll check the connection.

430
00:32:48,767 --> 00:32:50,531
This was no defect, sir.

431
00:32:50,702 --> 00:32:55,197
Someone or something
set off this alarm.

432
00:32:55,841 --> 00:32:57,935
Are you sure you didn't
walk through the living room?

433
00:32:58,109 --> 00:32:59,941
I told you, I was asleep.

434
00:33:00,111 --> 00:33:01,511
Bobby!

435
00:33:01,680 --> 00:33:06,880
I think I found it. I think I
got the culprit right here.

436
00:33:07,052 --> 00:33:10,216
This little guy was
crawling across our beam.

437
00:33:19,731 --> 00:33:22,462
Your security
system was your alibi.

438
00:33:22,634 --> 00:33:26,765
You brought the cat to the
office the day of the murder.

439
00:33:26,938 --> 00:33:29,999
It was the movement of the cat
that set off the motion detector.

440
00:33:30,175 --> 00:33:32,735
Well, that is very good, Ben.

441
00:33:33,111 --> 00:33:36,673
I had Forensics go through
your office. They found cat hairs.

442
00:33:36,848 --> 00:33:39,181
Well, you didn't have to
go to all that trouble, Ben.

443
00:33:39,351 --> 00:33:42,031
I could've told you Mo was with
me. I take him to work all the time.

444
00:33:45,223 --> 00:33:47,454
So let's see where we're at.

445
00:33:47,626 --> 00:33:49,254
You know that the
cat was in the office,

446
00:33:49,427 --> 00:33:51,692
but you can't prove that
I wasn't there with him.

447
00:33:51,863 --> 00:33:54,196
- Can you?
- No.

448
00:33:56,401 --> 00:33:57,699
Well, what's your point then?

449
00:33:57,869 --> 00:34:00,429
I mean, you can always
tell the owner of the building

450
00:34:00,605 --> 00:34:02,073
that I take my cat
to work with me,

451
00:34:02,240 --> 00:34:05,802
but that's not gonna get me in much
trouble. I own the building. Ha, ha, ha.

452
00:34:08,213 --> 00:34:11,581
You know, it never
ceases to amaze me

453
00:34:11,750 --> 00:34:14,413
how people never live
up to their reputations.

454
00:34:17,489 --> 00:34:21,449
Now here you are, one of the best
criminal lawyers that money can buy.

455
00:34:22,827 --> 00:34:23,988
Uh-uh.

456
00:34:24,629 --> 00:34:27,963
I don't get it. Why
are you doing this?

457
00:34:28,133 --> 00:34:32,764
Did I do something to you?
Did I do something to hurt you?

458
00:34:32,938 --> 00:34:35,169
There's got to be a reason.

459
00:34:36,942 --> 00:34:38,911
Does there, Ben?

460
00:34:54,793 --> 00:34:57,092
Malcolm Engle

461
00:34:58,096 --> 00:35:03,535
controlled every
element of this murder.

462
00:35:04,102 --> 00:35:06,765
He knew the guy
would be out of his office.

463
00:35:06,938 --> 00:35:09,305
He could leave as long
a message as he wanted.

464
00:35:09,474 --> 00:35:13,775
He knew his cat would keep moving
around, activate the motion detector.

465
00:35:13,945 --> 00:35:16,676
He knew his secretary
wouldn't see the file

466
00:35:16,848 --> 00:35:19,841
he slipped under the
door till she stood up.

467
00:35:20,018 --> 00:35:22,647
- Know what I don't get?
- What?

468
00:35:22,821 --> 00:35:25,814
Have you ever known Bob
Brooks to be late for anything?

469
00:35:25,991 --> 00:35:27,482
No.

470
00:35:27,659 --> 00:35:30,891
- He was often early.
- Right.

471
00:35:31,062 --> 00:35:33,293
So how do you suppose
Malcolm Engle knew

472
00:35:33,465 --> 00:35:37,459
Bob wasn't gonna get to
practice early like usual?

473
00:35:43,041 --> 00:35:44,771
Yeah, I drove Bob
to the park that day.

474
00:35:44,943 --> 00:35:47,412
Something was wrong
with his car. It wouldn't start.

475
00:35:47,579 --> 00:35:49,104
Has anybody used it since?

476
00:35:49,280 --> 00:35:52,717
The way things work around
here, I doubt it's been fixed.

477
00:35:52,884 --> 00:35:54,750
Let's give it a shot.

478
00:36:06,064 --> 00:36:07,930
Lift her up.

479
00:36:19,944 --> 00:36:21,674
Mr. Matlock?

480
00:36:21,846 --> 00:36:25,476
Defense calls Malcolm
Engle to the stand.

481
00:36:32,190 --> 00:36:38,391
Remember the night I came to your
house and you invited me to dinner?

482
00:36:39,798 --> 00:36:42,267
- Quite clearly.
- It was on that occasion

483
00:36:42,434 --> 00:36:45,495
that you challenged
me to solve a murder

484
00:36:45,670 --> 00:36:48,265
that you planned to
commit the next day.

485
00:36:49,674 --> 00:36:52,906
Now, that's an outrageous thing
to say. I never said any such thing.

486
00:36:53,078 --> 00:36:58,449
You didn't tell me that you were gonna
commit a murder at 3:15 the next day?

487
00:36:58,616 --> 00:37:01,484
No, I did not. And I
don't find this amusing.

488
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:04,446
You murdered my friend.

489
00:37:06,691 --> 00:37:10,890
Sir. Your Honor, I have
dedicated most of my adult life

490
00:37:11,062 --> 00:37:12,894
to helping those
less fortunate than I.

491
00:37:13,064 --> 00:37:16,159
I resent this man
slandering me in public.

492
00:37:16,735 --> 00:37:19,364
The witness has a
point, Mr. Matlock.

493
00:37:20,705 --> 00:37:23,869
Let me reword the question.

494
00:37:24,042 --> 00:37:28,673
Are you saying that you
did not murder Bob Brooks?

495
00:37:28,847 --> 00:37:30,907
Yes, of course. That's
exactly what I'm saying.

496
00:37:31,082 --> 00:37:34,780
- I didn't know the man.
- You're a creature of habit.

497
00:37:35,787 --> 00:37:38,154
- You could say that, I guess.
- Let me guess.

498
00:37:38,323 --> 00:37:40,203
- Mr. Matlock...
- Figure of speech, Your Honor.

499
00:37:40,325 --> 00:37:42,165
He said "guess," I said
"guess." I'm on my way.

500
00:37:42,327 --> 00:37:45,024
- Go on.
- I bet... A small supposition.

501
00:37:46,197 --> 00:37:49,634
I bet you eat the same
breakfast every day,

502
00:37:49,801 --> 00:37:53,101
buy your clothes at
the same men's store.

503
00:37:53,271 --> 00:37:57,299
I bet you even have a
standing haircut appointment.

504
00:37:57,475 --> 00:38:00,035
Your Honor, first he accuses
the witness of murder,

505
00:38:00,211 --> 00:38:02,737
and now he wants to know
how often he gets his hair cut.

506
00:38:02,914 --> 00:38:04,246
Where are we going with this?

507
00:38:04,415 --> 00:38:07,044
Your Honor, this man
committed murder.

508
00:38:07,218 --> 00:38:09,653
I need a certain
latitude to prove it.

509
00:38:09,821 --> 00:38:11,255
Mr. Matlock...

510
00:38:11,422 --> 00:38:15,689
If I don't prove it, I'll step
aside for the rest of my life.

511
00:38:20,398 --> 00:38:22,230
Go ahead.

512
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:27,031
Now, I said I bet you have a
standing haircut appointment.

513
00:38:27,205 --> 00:38:28,229
How about it?

514
00:38:28,807 --> 00:38:30,935
I get my hair cut
every two weeks.

515
00:38:31,442 --> 00:38:35,436
- Where?
- At the Don Ross Salon.

516
00:38:36,614 --> 00:38:41,552
To be accurate, that used
to be true, till six months ago.

517
00:38:41,719 --> 00:38:46,783
You haven't been in the Don Ross
Salon in almost half a year, have you?

518
00:38:48,560 --> 00:38:52,395
I have the appointment
book from the salon right here.

519
00:39:04,776 --> 00:39:06,745
The truth is

520
00:39:07,579 --> 00:39:10,208
you don't get your hair
cut anymore, do you?

521
00:39:12,183 --> 00:39:15,119
That's a hairpiece
you're wearing, isn't it?

522
00:39:18,623 --> 00:39:21,855
You don't have any hair

523
00:39:22,026 --> 00:39:24,552
because of your
chemotherapy treatments.

524
00:39:24,729 --> 00:39:26,220
Isn't that correct?

525
00:39:29,133 --> 00:39:30,863
Mr. Engle?

526
00:39:32,337 --> 00:39:36,331
You have cancer and you're
terminally ill, aren't you?

527
00:39:42,947 --> 00:39:46,714
Mr. Engle, I must ask you
to answer that question.

528
00:39:48,620 --> 00:39:53,024
The doctors tell me I have
three to six months to live.

529
00:39:55,360 --> 00:39:57,761
That seems so unfair.

530
00:39:58,229 --> 00:40:04,829
A man like you who's devoted
his life to charity and doing good.

531
00:40:05,470 --> 00:40:09,430
It must be infuriating
that others should live.

532
00:40:12,844 --> 00:40:14,938
Life isn't fair.

533
00:40:16,748 --> 00:40:20,844
I suppose at a time like this
one wishes to make peace

534
00:40:21,019 --> 00:40:24,888
where one can and
close the book on a life.

535
00:40:26,958 --> 00:40:28,358
I suppose so. Yeah. Mm-hm.

536
00:40:28,526 --> 00:40:34,955
And I suppose it's a time when
one wishes to settle old scores.

537
00:40:36,534 --> 00:40:39,026
I'm not sure I know
what you're referring to.

538
00:40:40,772 --> 00:40:45,472
From the time I had dinner at
your house, this was personal

539
00:40:45,843 --> 00:40:47,209
between you and me.

540
00:40:50,248 --> 00:40:53,844
My associates searched
every case I ever tried

541
00:40:54,018 --> 00:40:56,146
looking for a
connection between us.

542
00:40:56,621 --> 00:41:00,388
They didn't find anything. They
went back to college, law school.

543
00:41:00,558 --> 00:41:01,890
Nothing showed up.

544
00:41:02,894 --> 00:41:07,559
I began to think there was
no connection between us.

545
00:41:07,732 --> 00:41:12,693
But behind that
placid, quiet facade

546
00:41:12,870 --> 00:41:18,707
there was so much hate and
anger towards me, we looked again.

547
00:41:22,780 --> 00:41:26,182
Have you ever been
married, Mr. Engle?

548
00:41:26,351 --> 00:41:27,683
No.

549
00:41:27,852 --> 00:41:29,548
Do you have any children?

550
00:41:30,188 --> 00:41:31,622
No.

551
00:41:31,789 --> 00:41:34,224
Did you ever have children?

552
00:41:36,294 --> 00:41:37,785
That's not a difficult question.

553
00:41:37,962 --> 00:41:40,955
Please, did you
ever have children?

554
00:41:42,567 --> 00:41:47,164
Yes, I had a son. His
name was Jason Lange.

555
00:41:48,539 --> 00:41:52,943
You never married his mother.
Your son, what happened to him?

556
00:41:56,547 --> 00:41:59,176
They were both killed
in a fast food restaurant

557
00:41:59,350 --> 00:42:03,378
that some lunatic
decided to shoot up.

558
00:42:04,622 --> 00:42:07,558
The man whom you
describe as a lunatic

559
00:42:07,725 --> 00:42:12,527
was an out-of-work war veteran
with severe emotional problems.

560
00:42:12,697 --> 00:42:17,397
Until yesterday I did not know
it was your boy that he killed.

561
00:42:18,903 --> 00:42:22,840
I defended that man and pled
him guilty by reason of insanity,

562
00:42:23,007 --> 00:42:24,976
and he was institutionalized.

563
00:42:25,143 --> 00:42:28,170
You never forgave
me for that, did you?

564
00:42:29,714 --> 00:42:32,843
That's why you killed
someone so close to me.

565
00:42:33,017 --> 00:42:34,781
You wanted me to feel helpless.

566
00:42:34,952 --> 00:42:39,686
You wanted me to experience
what you had experienced, didn't you?

567
00:42:43,294 --> 00:42:47,356
I had nothing to do with
the death of Bob Brooks.

568
00:42:51,335 --> 00:42:55,670
You made it appear that
you were in your office

569
00:42:56,207 --> 00:42:58,972
at the time he died.

570
00:42:59,143 --> 00:43:00,543
I was in my office.

571
00:43:00,711 --> 00:43:05,206
Wasn't it your cat that
activated the motion detector?

572
00:43:05,983 --> 00:43:08,817
- The cat was in my office. So was I.
- Now, you were on the phone

573
00:43:08,986 --> 00:43:11,956
talking to an answer machine
of a business associate.

574
00:43:12,123 --> 00:43:17,118
Yes, I was. You remember
saying to me, "Catch me if you can"?

575
00:43:17,862 --> 00:43:23,267
You controlled every
aspect of Bob Brooks' murder.

576
00:43:23,434 --> 00:43:25,300
That's why you had
the cat in the office.

577
00:43:25,470 --> 00:43:31,034
That's why you used a tape of your
voice to talk to that answer machine.

578
00:43:33,644 --> 00:43:39,709
You plotted every detail
of Bob Brooks' murder.

579
00:43:39,884 --> 00:43:43,719
You were even aware
of one of his trademarks,

580
00:43:43,888 --> 00:43:48,258
Bob Brooks was always
on time, often early,

581
00:43:48,426 --> 00:43:51,555
for everything in his life.

582
00:43:52,697 --> 00:43:57,658
But in order for you to
commit this murder at the time

583
00:43:57,835 --> 00:44:00,828
and the place you
predicted to me,

584
00:44:01,339 --> 00:44:05,242
he had to come late for
softball practice that day.

585
00:44:05,409 --> 00:44:09,870
Are you aware that Bob
Brooks did not drive his car

586
00:44:10,047 --> 00:44:11,913
to the park the day he died?

587
00:44:13,017 --> 00:44:14,679
No, I'm not.

588
00:44:15,419 --> 00:44:18,947
Another officer, Greg
Charles, gave him a lift

589
00:44:19,123 --> 00:44:21,251
because his car wouldn't start.

590
00:44:27,665 --> 00:44:29,896
This distributor cap

591
00:44:30,067 --> 00:44:35,404
was removed from Bob
Brooks' car yesterday afternoon.

592
00:44:35,573 --> 00:44:37,974
One of the cables
had been disconnected.

593
00:44:38,142 --> 00:44:40,407
Impossible to start it.

594
00:44:40,578 --> 00:44:45,243
I must say, forgive my
profanity, you did a helluva job.

595
00:44:46,150 --> 00:44:49,587
But the best laid
plans often go awry.

596
00:44:49,754 --> 00:44:51,848
You did everything right.

597
00:44:52,523 --> 00:44:56,187
Probably put on a mechanic's
uniform so you wouldn't stick out.

598
00:44:56,360 --> 00:45:02,300
You knew you couldn't use gloves
in a police parking lot, conspicuous.

599
00:45:02,466 --> 00:45:09,305
So you used a cloth to remove
the distributor cap and the cable.

600
00:45:09,473 --> 00:45:16,073
Then when you were done, you
very carefully used that rag to wipe off

601
00:45:16,247 --> 00:45:21,845
any prints you might have left
on the hood and on the parts.

602
00:45:22,019 --> 00:45:24,488
You did a fine job.

603
00:45:27,625 --> 00:45:29,594
But you missed one.

604
00:45:30,928 --> 00:45:35,923
One print, the index finger.

605
00:45:38,336 --> 00:45:40,601
Best laid plans...

606
00:45:42,707 --> 00:45:45,939
You wanted Bob
Brooks to come late.

607
00:45:46,110 --> 00:45:50,172
He had to come late for
you to commit this murder

608
00:45:50,348 --> 00:45:55,787
at the place and the
time that you predicted.

609
00:45:55,953 --> 00:45:59,481
And then you coldly and
brutally murdered him.

610
00:46:03,894 --> 00:46:06,796
A man you didn't even know.

611
00:46:07,932 --> 00:46:11,232
All for revenge against me.

612
00:46:15,239 --> 00:46:20,109
What are you gonna do, Ben?
Get me the death sentence?

613
00:46:25,149 --> 00:46:27,584
I've already got it.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
